# {{PROJECT_NAME}}

**Read `.github/AGENT_RULES.md` before making any change in this repo.**
It is the binding rulebook (review tiers, git rules, secrets, Definition
of Done) — this file only exists to make sure that one actually gets
read, since nothing about a fresh session guarantees it otherwise.

Copy this to the project root as `CLAUDE.md` (Claude Code auto-loads
this filename) — or `AGENTS.md` if your primary coding agent uses that
convention instead; several tools are converging on it. If more than one
agent/tool is in use on this project, keep both filenames present with
the same content rather than picking one.

## Why this file exists at all

This kit depends entirely on an agent actually reading
`AGENT_RULES.md` — but nothing forces that to happen automatically
unless the agent's own tooling auto-loads *something* at session start.
This file is that something. A rule that only lives in a document
nobody's tooling opens automatically is a rule that gets missed, not
because it's wrong, but because it was never in view — see this kit's
own `LESSONS-LEARNED.md` ("A rule that isn't visible at the point of
action gets ignored, however well-written").

## What to do at the start of a session

1. If you haven't already this session, read `.github/AGENT_RULES.md`
   in full — not a summary of it from memory.
2. Check whether this clone's setup is actually current: run `sh
   scripts/verify-setup.sh` if it exists in this repo (it's read-only —
   safe to run any time, it never changes anything). If it reports a
   failure, walk the human through fixing it before doing anything else
   that touches git or this project's governed tooling — don't silently
   work around a broken setup.
3. If `scripts/verify-setup.sh` doesn't exist yet in this repo, tell the
   human plainly rather than silently continuing — but don't assume it
   means setup was done wrong. Two different real causes, worth telling
   apart: either this kit's setup really was only partially applied
   (see `SETUP-GUIDE.md` in `taseenali/Kit-GO`), **or**
   this project adopted an older version of this kit, from before
   `verify-setup.sh` existed (check `CHANGELOG.md` for when it was
   added, and this project's own record of which version it adopted, if
   one exists) — in which case the real fix is upgrading the kit
   (`SETUP-GUIDE.md`'s "Upgrading an already-adopted project" section),
   not redoing setup that was already correct for its time.
4. Check `docs/OPEN-RESEARCH-QUEUE.md` (if this project uses one) before
   starting any pass sized beyond a small fix, per `AGENT_RULES.md`'s
   Definition of Done — this holds regardless of whether
   `verify-setup.sh` warned about it: a missing warning (the script
   didn't run, doesn't exist yet, or its check is inert for a project
   that keeps this file somewhere other than `docs/`) is not the same as
   "nothing open." Treat a real `verify-setup.sh` warning as a reminder
   that reinforces this step, never as the thing that turns it on. A
   warning about unresolved `docs/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md` entries is
   different in kind, not just in urgency: those are deferred kit
   bugs/ideas for the kit maintainer's own later attention, not something
   to act on in this session — reading them costs nothing and keeps you
   honest about what's actually pending, but they don't gate the work in
   front of you the way an open research question does. This is this
   kit's real answer to AI agents having no memory across sessions: a
   written record only helps if it actually gets read back, not just
   written once and trusted to be remembered next time.

## Kit-GO commands

If `docs/KIT-GO.md` exists in this repo, the Kit-GO command set is
installed. **Typing `Kit-GO` (or running `/kit-go`) changes nothing**: it
re-reads the rules and checks the repo's health (on a branch you didn't
write, do the playbook's integrity diff first). Use it
at the start of a session and whenever you lose the thread (after a long
stretch of work, or after your context was compacted). Other commands:
`Kit-GO help` (list them all), `Kit-GO guide` (quick guide), `Kit-GO guide
me` (guide for this project's current stage), `Kit-GO log` (record a Kit
bug or idea), `Kit-GO sync` (plan an upgrade). The full playbook, including
how an agent without slash commands should treat these typed phrases, is
`docs/KIT-GO.md` -- read the section for a command before running it. Kit-GO
never approves anything: tier rules and human sign-off are unchanged.

